     1,
     "<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">Hi,</span></p><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><br></span></p><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">I am currently attempting to simulate binary exogenous data using\r\nlavaan. I’ve pared my example down to the basic concepts, but am attempting to\r\napply these concepts to a far more complex SEM model with multiple binary predictors.</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\"><br></span></p><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">The model I’m using to simulate data is:</span><br></p><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">PopModel &lt;- &quot;</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">  X | 0*t1      !X threshold &amp; mean at 0 for 50/50\r\nsplit</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">  X ~ 0*1</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">  Y ~ .61*X    !beta coefficient</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">  X ~~ 1*X</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">  Y ~~ .6279*Y \r\n!residual variance of Y</span><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">&quot;</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">I simulated 100,000 observations to assess the accuracy of\r\nthe simulated data and found that the covariance and correlation matrices in\r\nthis population-level dataset are underestimating the expected effect (tested across multiple starting seeds). Below\r\nare my syntax and results.</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">SampData &lt;- simulateData(PopModel,</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">                         sample.nobs = 100000,</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">                         seed = 1234)</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">fitted(sem(PopModel))</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">$cov</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\"> \r\nY    X   </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">Y 1.00    \r\n</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">X 0.61 1.00</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">$mean</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">Y X </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">0 0 </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">$th</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">X|t1 </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">   0 </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">round(cov(SampData), 3)</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<span style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">      Y     X</span></span><div><span style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">Y 0.999 0.243</span></span></div><div><span style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">X 0.243 0.250</span></span>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"ES\" style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">round(cor(SampData), 3)</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<span style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">      Y     X</span></span></div><div><span style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">Y 1.000 0.485</span></span></div><div><span style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black\">X 0.485 1.000</span></span>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">The simulated dataset shows that the binary\r\nunstandardized variance is .25, which is what I would expect for a binary\r\nvariable with a 50/50 distribution (.5*.5). However, the correlation/covariance\r\ncoefficients between X and Y are much lower than expected.</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">I’ve dug around a bit, and asked a similar question on the\r\nsimsem documentation, and Terrence informed me that this is likely because the\r\nX variable is simulated at the latent response level. Since variables are\r\nstandardized, simulated data in this example reflect the following interpretation: <i>for every one SD\r\nincrease in X, there is a .61 SD increase in Y</i>. This is not what I’m wanting to\r\nobtain when simulating data with a binary exogenous variables. Instead, I’m hoping to simulate data\r\nthat has the following interpretation: <i>for every <b>one-unit</b>\r\nincrease in X, there is a .61 SD increase in Y</i>. This is the standard approach for interpretation of a binary variable in regression analysis. </span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">This leads me to my question. Is there a way—using lavaan\r\nsyntax (or by changing any of the population-produced matrices)—to simulate binary exogenous data in a more practical manner, where B could be interpreted as <i>for every <b>one-unit\r\n(not one-SD)</b> increase in X, there is a B-SD increase in Y</i>? Or is there a\r\nway to adjust the expected B parameter of interest in the population model, to correct\r\nfor the normal-to-binary under-estimation of B that seems to happen during data\r\ngeneration? </span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">I cannot seem to find another way to do this in lavaan even after closely\r\nexamining current documentation, aside from using multigroup analysis, which I am\r\nhoping to avoid due to the complexity of the full model. I\r\nalso know that simulating this type of data is possible with matrix notation in\r\nsimsem but am hoping to use that as a last alternative if possible. Any insight moving forward would be most\r\nhelpful.</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">Thanks,</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">Kaylee</span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p><div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "KDblFxTCDAAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2023-02-26T09:09:36Z",
   "subject": "Re: Simulating Binary Exogenous Data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi Kaylee,<br><br><div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">I’m hoping to simulate data\r\nthat has the following interpretation: <i>for every <b>one-unit</b>\r\nincrease in X, there is a .61 SD increase in Y</i>. This is the standard approach for interpretation of a binary variable in regression analysis.</span></p></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div>That is what I provided links to do in our discussion on GitHub:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://github.com/simsem/simsem/issues/81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/simsem/simsem/issues/81&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029366000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2uZymA7ov9PwWGCEY5D3sQ\">https://github.com/simsem/simsem/issues/81</a></div><div><br></div><div>There is a larger tutorial associated with the second link, which explains more fully how to use the simsem-matrix approach to simulate data with binary exogenous variables.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-022-01996-0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-022-01996-0&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029366000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0i1TWr3x4OZEh-5yoxmpX-\">https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-022-01996-0</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>The context there was Monte Carlo power analysis for moderated mediation models.  But the same concepts generalize to other Monte Carlo simulations using other models with categorical exogenous variables.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><div><br></div><div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"> </span><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">Is there a way—using lavaan\r\nsyntax </span></p></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div>Not directly.  In this case, simsem simply passes your population model syntax to<font face=\"Courier New\"> lavaan::simulateData()</font>, which internally calculates model-implied Mu and Sigma to call <font face=\"Courier New\">MASS::mvrnorm()</font>.</div><div><br></div><div>If you are only simulating dummy-coded predictors, you could use multiple-group model syntax, so that the effect of X manifests as differences in endogenous-variable intercepts across groups.  The tutorial paper I linked to above also shows this approach.  However, that would get quite complicated with multiple X variables, because you have to collectively treat all the combinations of groups as arising from 1 grouping variable.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><div><br></div><div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">(or by changing any of the population-produced matrices)—to simulate binary exogenous data in a more practical manner, where B could be interpreted as </span><i style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">for every <b>one-unit\r\n(not one-SD)</b> increase in X, there is a B-SD increase in Y</i><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">? Or is there a\r\nway to adjust the expected B parameter of interest in the population model, to correct\r\nfor the normal-to-binary under-estimation of B that seems to happen during data\r\ngeneration?</span></p></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div>Hmmm, maybe you could convert your group-mean difference (unstandardized effect of dummy-coded X) to the X-Y point-biserial correlation.  Than that can be converted to the biserial correlation, calculated on the assumption that X is a discrete manifestation of a latent X*.  You could specify the biserial correlation in the syntax (assuming you correctly specify Y&#39;s residual variance such that its total variance = 1), then discretize after.  Olsson&#39;s (1982) paper shows the formula linking the polyserial and point-polyserial correlations.  The biserial versions are just special cases when data are dichotomized.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02294164\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02294164&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029366000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ovd8N1YHw6FGgignBlrJz\">https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02294164</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>In another tutorial, I capitalized on this transformation in a different context (maybe still relevant for your study?).  I reproduce the formula in my paper as Eq. 16, which is greatly simplified in your case because your category weights are 0 and 1, your threshold is 0, and you already know your population <i>SD</i> = 0.5 for a dummy code with balanced groups.  The only extra work is inverting that equation so you solve for the biserial (rho_LRV) for the given point-biserial (rho-tilde).  So once you convert your group-mean difference to a point-biserial, you multiply it by the reciprocal of the right-hand dividend in my Eq. 16 (Olsson&#39;s Eq. 11) to get the biserial you could use in syntax.</div><div><br></div><div>However, I think that is only a feasible solution with a single X variable.  With multiple X, you would be working with partial correlations.  This is still possible, but I expect more complicated than simply working with simsem-matrices to directly specify population effects of exogenous covariates in the metric you intend.</div><div><br></div><div><div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029366000&amp;usg=AOvVaw229hF0bP_pv8rRpekH8XaQ\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "q6wpVCfCDAAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2023-02-26T09:10:59Z",
   "subject": "Re: Simulating Binary Exogenous Data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "woops, I forgot to link to my tutorial with Eq. 16 (if you are interested): <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2021.1988609\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2021.1988609&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029367000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3sgh9IW4rME_y4nUbb4f6R\">https://doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2021.1988609</a><div>But again, I think simsem matrices are your best approach.<br><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>In another tutorial, I capitalized on this transformation in a different context (maybe still relevant for your study?).  I reproduce the formula in my paper as Eq. 16, which is greatly simplified in your case because your category weights are 0 and 1, your threshold is 0, and you already know your population <i>SD</i> = 0.5 for a dummy code with balanced groups.  The only extra work is inverting that equation so you solve for the biserial (rho_LRV) for the given point-biserial (rho-tilde).  So once you convert your group-mean difference to a point-biserial, you multiply it by the reciprocal of the right-hand dividend in my Eq. 16 (Olsson&#39;s Eq. 11) to get the biserial you could use in syntax.</div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "gd0pv6zSAgAJ",
   "from": "Kaylee Litson",
   "author": "19bf7d5e244f398d",
   "date": "2023-05-16T00:03:18Z",
   "subject": "Re: Simulating Binary Exogenous Data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks very much for all of your help on this Terrence! Just to provide an update: simsem matrices were much, much easier to use than trying to adjust for and incorporate transformed correlations, in case anyone else is running into the same issue. </div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sun, Feb 26, 2023 at 2:09 AM Terrence Jorgensen &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tjorge...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">Hi Kaylee,<br><br><div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10.5pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Roboto;color:black;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">I’m hoping to simulate data\r\nthat has the following interpretation: <i>for every <b>one-unit</b>\r\nincrease in X, there is a .61 SD increase in Y</i>. This is the standard approach for interpretation of a binary variable in regression analysis.</span></p></blockquote><div><br></div><div>That is what I provided links to do in our discussion on GitHub:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://github.com/simsem/simsem/issues/81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/simsem/simsem/issues/81&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029371000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3dPcxqTelJztyk3c62oIg0\">https://github.com/simsem/simsem/issues/81</a></div><div><br></div><div>There is a larger tutorial associated with the second link, which explains more fully how to use the simsem-matrix approach to simulate data with binary exogenous variables.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-022-01996-0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-022-01996-0&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029371000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Rai0lquFLxbEDSRyjgt6C\">https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-022-01996-0</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>The context there was Monte Carlo power analysis for moderated mediation models.  But the same concepts gener