div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><p style=\"margin:0in;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">(or by changing any of the population-produced matrices)—to simulate binary exogenous data in a more practical manner, where B could be interpreted as </span><i style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">for every <b>one-unit\r\n(not one-SD)</b> increase in X, there is a B-SD increase in Y</i><span style=\"color:black;font-family:Roboto;font-size:10.5pt\">? Or is there a\r\nway to adjust the expected B parameter of interest in the population model, to correct\r\nfor the normal-to-binary under-estimation of B that seems to happen during data\r\ngeneration?</span></p></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Hmmm, maybe you could convert your group-mean difference (unstandardized effect of dummy-coded X) to the X-Y point-biserial correlation.  Than that can be converted to the biserial correlation, calculated on the assumption that X is a discrete manifestation of a latent X*.  You could specify the biserial correlation in the syntax (assuming you correctly specify Y&#39;s residual variance such that its total variance = 1), then discretize after.  Olsson&#39;s (1982) paper shows the formula linking the polyserial and point-polyserial correlations.  The biserial versions are just special cases when data are dichotomized.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02294164\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02294164&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029371000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3hP6-9-KBq_11QnQW2Qioe\">https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02294164</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>In another tutorial, I capitalized on this transformation in a different context (maybe still relevant for your study?).  I reproduce the formula in my paper as Eq. 16, which is greatly simplified in your case because your category weights are 0 and 1, your threshold is 0, and you already know your population <i>SD</i> = 0.5 for a dummy code with balanced groups.  The only extra work is inverting that equation so you solve for the biserial (rho_LRV) for the given point-biserial (rho-tilde).  So once you convert your group-mean difference to a point-biserial, you multiply it by the reciprocal of the right-hand dividend in my Eq. 16 (Olsson&#39;s Eq. 11) to get the biserial you could use in syntax.</div><div><br></div><div>However, I think that is only a feasible solution with a single X variable.  With multiple X, you would be working with partial correlations.  This is still possible, but I expect more complicated than simply working with simsem-matrices to directly specify population effects of exogenous covariates in the metric you intend.</div><div><br></div><div><div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792416029371000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3YcDBE1mv3FmzyE1wfb-PV\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div style=\"margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><br></div></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
    ],
